```
MCP Long-Lived Subscription Authorization Drift (Track 3)

Placeholder source: index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json -- not shipped by
Splunk by default. See detections/field-mapping.md.

This is the AUTHORITATIVE implementation of Track 3 (the Sigma correlation in
detections/sigma/mcp_subscription_drift_correlation.yml cannot faithfully express this -- see
that file and detections/README.md for why). Encodes all four parts of the locked condition:
  1. a subscription exists and is/was active,
  2. authorization became invalid (authoritative revocation effective_at, OR silent expiry via
     mcp.authz.valid_until when no revocation event exists at all),
  3. a notification was delivered strictly AFTER that boundary,
  4. no mcp.subscription.close event exists at or before that notification's timestamp.

ORDERING MATTERS: this deliberately compares event timestamps/field values with real
inequality logic (notif_time > boundary_time, earliest_close_time > notif_time), not mere
co-occurrence ("notification and revocation exist somewhere").

ASSUMPTION: ingestion is configured (e.g. via props.conf TIME_PREFIX/TIME_FORMAT) so that
Splunk's internal _time reflects each event's own `timestamp` field. The strptime() format
string below assumes ISO-8601 with millisecond precision and a literal "Z" suffix
(2026-09-05T10:10:00.000Z) for the *other* timestamp-valued fields carried inside the JSON
payload (effective_at, valid_until) -- these are ordinary string field values, not _time, and
must be parsed explicitly. Validate this format string against real ingested data; it has not
been executed against a live Splunk instance in this project (see detections/README.md,
"validation tooling used").

detected_at-only timing (mcp.authz.change.timing_confidence = "detected_only") is deliberately
NEVER promoted into this high-confidence search -- see the separate, clearly-labeled
low-confidence search at the bottom.

BLOCK 6 FIX (docs/validation-report.md, fixture V5-03): only mcp.authz.change.type values that
actually invalidate authorization ("revoked", "expired", "scope_downgraded") are treated as a
boundary. "scope_upgraded" (a renewal/expansion of access) is explicitly excluded -- the
original search ignored change type entirely and false-positived on a legitimate renewal that
happened to be authoritative.

FIXES APPLIED IN THIS PASS (see docs/validation-report.md for the full write-up):
  (a) The expiry leg's inner join now requires BOTH subscription_id AND principal_hash to
      match (it previously joined on subscription_id alone, which was a real inconsistency
      with the KQL implementation and could, in principle, match a notification to a
      different principal's open/valid_until on the same subscription_id string).
      principal_hash is retained through the open-event subsearch specifically so this join
      can use it.
  (b) Every SPL `join type=inner` below now explicitly sets `max=0`. Splunk's `join` command
      defaults to `max=1` -- keeping only the FIRST matching subsearch row per outer-search
      row -- which would silently drop all but one authorization_change/open event when a
      principal or subscription has more than one (e.g., an early legitimate renewal followed
      later by a real revocation, or independently-applicable revocation AND expiry
      boundaries). `max=0` preserves every applicable match, producing one output row per
      (notification, applicable boundary) combination, exactly matching KQL's native
      whole-table join semantics. The aggregated CLOSE joins are left at Splunk's default
      (max=1 is sufficient there without change) because the close subsearch already
      collapses to at most one row per (subscription_id, principal_hash) via `stats min(...)`
      -- there is nothing for max=0 to preserve in that specific subsearch.
  (c) The close-suppression join now requires BOTH subscription_id AND principal_hash to
      match (previously subscription_id alone). mcp.subscription.id is only a per-connection
      JSON-RPC request id (Block 1 SS7) and is not guaranteed unique across different
      principals' connections -- a subscription_id-only join could let one principal's close
      event incorrectly suppress a DIFFERENT principal's genuine violation notification that
      happens to share the same subscription_id string. principal.id_hash is already a
      required field on mcp.subscription.close (telemetry/schema.md), so this uses existing,
      already-collected telemetry -- it invents nothing new, and does not affect the
      malformed-telemetry case a notification missing its OWN subscription_id represents
      (that case is unrelated to how close events are matched).

KNOWN, UNRESOLVED RISK (NOT fixed in this pass -- see detections/README.md "remaining risks"):
the revocation leg's join is intentionally scoped by principal_hash ONLY (authorization_change
events carry no subscription id at all -- there is nothing else to join on). A principal
holding two or more concurrent subscriptions, one revoked and one still legitimately valid,
could have the still-valid subscription's notifications incorrectly matched against the
other's revocation boundary. Scoping this join by subscription_id as well would remove that
risk but would make a notification missing mcp.subscription.id (malformed telemetry)
impossible to correlate at all. This tradeoff is reported, not silently resolved.
```

`comment("Leg A: authoritative revocation drift (Block 4 A12, A14)")`
index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json "event.name"="mcp.subscription.notification"
| rename "mcp.subscription.id" as subscription_id, "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash,
    "mcp.subscription.notification_type" as notification_type
| eval notif_time=_time
| join type=inner max=0 principal_hash
    [ search index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json
        "event.name"="mcp.subscription.authorization_change"
        "mcp.authz.change.timing_confidence"="authoritative"
        ("mcp.authz.change.type"="revoked" OR "mcp.authz.change.type"="expired" OR "mcp.authz.change.type"="scope_downgraded")
      | rename "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash,
          "mcp.authz.change.type" as change_type, "mcp.authz.change.source" as change_source
      | eval effective_at=strptime('mcp.authz.change.effective_at', "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%3QZ")
      | fields principal_hash effective_at change_type change_source ]
| where notif_time > effective_at
| join type=left subscription_id principal_hash
    [ search index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json "event.name"="mcp.subscription.close"
      | rename "mcp.subscription.id" as subscription_id, "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash
      | eval close_time=_time
      | stats min(close_time) as earliest_close_time by subscription_id, principal_hash ]
| where isnull(earliest_close_time) OR earliest_close_time > notif_time
| eval Boundary="effective_at", BoundaryTime=strftime(effective_at, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%3QZ")
| eval Confidence="high"
| table notif_time subscription_id principal_hash notification_type Boundary BoundaryTime
    change_type change_source Confidence
| append
    [ `comment("Leg B: silent expiry drift (Block 4 A13 -- no authorization_change event exists at all)")`
      search index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json "event.name"="mcp.subscription.notification"
      | rename "mcp.subscription.id" as subscription_id, "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash,
          "mcp.subscription.notification_type" as notification_type
      | eval notif_time=_time
      | join type=inner max=0 subscription_id principal_hash
          [ search index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json "event.name"="mcp.subscription.open"
              "mcp.authz.valid_until"=*
            | rename "mcp.subscription.id" as subscription_id, "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash
            | eval valid_until=strptime('mcp.authz.valid_until', "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%3QZ")
            | fields subscription_id principal_hash valid_until ]
      | where notif_time > valid_until
      | join type=left subscription_id principal_hash
          [ search index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json "event.name"="mcp.subscription.close"
            | rename "mcp.subscription.id" as subscription_id, "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash
            | eval close_time=_time
            | stats min(close_time) as earliest_close_time by subscription_id, principal_hash ]
      | where isnull(earliest_close_time) OR earliest_close_time > notif_time
      | eval Boundary="valid_until", BoundaryTime=strftime(valid_until, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%3QZ")
      | eval change_type="expired", change_source="token_expiry_computed", Confidence="high"
      | table notif_time subscription_id principal_hash notification_type Boundary BoundaryTime
          change_type change_source Confidence ]
| eval Severity="High", DetectionTrack="Track3_SubscriptionAuthorizationDrift"
| sort 0 notif_time

```
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOW-CONFIDENCE / INFORMATIONAL ONLY -- detected_at-only timing (Block 4 A15). Per the locked
timing-confidence model, a notification observed after detected_at (with no authoritative
effective_at anywhere) is NOT promoted to a high-confidence Track 3 verdict: the true change
moment is unknown and could be before or after the notification. Kept as a separate saved
search for hunting/context; never merged into the primary search above.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json "event.name"="mcp.subscription.notification"
| rename "mcp.subscription.id" as subscription_id, "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash,
    "mcp.subscription.notification_type" as notification_type
| eval notif_time=_time
| join type=inner max=0 principal_hash
    [ search index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json
        "event.name"="mcp.subscription.authorization_change"
        "mcp.authz.change.timing_confidence"="detected_only"
        ("mcp.authz.change.type"="revoked" OR "mcp.authz.change.type"="expired" OR "mcp.authz.change.type"="scope_downgraded")
      | rename "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash
      | eval detected_at=strptime('mcp.authz.change.detected_at', "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%3QZ")
      | fields principal_hash detected_at ]
| where notif_time > detected_at
| join type=left subscription_id principal_hash
    [ search index=mcp_security_audit sourcetype=mcp:audit:json "event.name"="mcp.subscription.close"
      | rename "mcp.subscription.id" as subscription_id, "principal.id_hash" as principal_hash
      | eval close_time=_time
      | stats min(close_time) as earliest_close_time by subscription_id, principal_hash ]
| where isnull(earliest_close_time) OR earliest_close_time > notif_time
| eval Confidence="low"
| eval Note="detected_at-only; true revocation time unknown -- do not treat as a confirmed violation"
| table notif_time subscription_id principal_hash notification_type Confidence Note
| sort 0 notif_time
```
